2026-09-02 23:13:37 [DEBUG] llm.engine: ARGS: ('\n        You are a workflow assistant. Extract parameters for the intent "select_idea_by_reference".\n\n        User message:\n        select this: Trump, President of U.S. v. Barbara\n\n        Parameter schema for this intent:\n        - "reference": type=string, required=True\n\n        Extraction instructions for this intent:\n        - The "reference" parameter must always be set to the idea\'s "id" field.\n- Never return the idea name or description.\n- Use the relevant context block (which lists ideas with id, name, and description)\n  to determine which idea the user is referring to.\n- If the user refers to an idea by name, description, or position (e.g., "the immigration case",\n  "idea 3", "the fed reserve governor case"), resolve it to the correct idea and return its id.\n\n\n\nRelevant context:\nideas:\n  - id: 25-365, name: Trump, President of U.S. v. Barbara, description: The case concerns the interpretation of the Citizenship Clause in the Constitution, specifically whether children born to non-citizens living temporarily in the United States should be granted citizenship.\n  - id: 24A884, name: Trump v. CASA, Inc., description: The case concerns the constitutionality of Executive Order 14,160, which protects American citizenship, and the use of universal injunctions by district courts to block government actions.\n  - id: 24-5438, name: Bowe v. United States, description: The case Bowe v. United States, docket number 24-5438, concerns the application of a federal statute governing habeas corpus petitions and motions to vacate convictions.\n  - id: 24-320, name: Soto v. United States, description: The Supreme Court is deciding whether the Combat-Related Special Compensation statute grants the secretary concerned authority to settle claims, making the Barring Act inapplicable. The dispute centers on whether the statute authorizes a specific government official to determine claim validity and amount due, which would exempt it from the Barring Act.\n  - id: 21-147, name: Egbert v. Boule, description: The Supreme Court is considering whether to expand a legal doctrine known as Bivens, which allows individuals to sue federal officials for damages in certain cases. The Court is weighing whether to apply Bivens to new types of claims, including First Amendment retaliation and Fourth Amendment border security cases.\n  - id: 23-370, name: Erlinger v. United States, description: The Supreme Court is deciding whether a judge can increase a defendant\'s sentencing range based on conduct not previously found by a jury, or if the Sixth Amendment requires a more specific factual inquiry.\n  - id: 22-179, name: United States v. Hansen, description: The case of United States v. Hansen concerns a century-old law making it a crime to encourage or induce certain immigration offenses, and whether the statute\'s broad language can be interpreted in a way that would allow its facial invalidation under the First Amendment.\n  - id: 21-1168, name: Mallory v. Norfolk Southern Railway Co., description: The case is about whether Pennsylvania\'s consent-by-registration statute complies with the Constitution, specifically the Fourteenth Amendment, and whether it unfairly favors large corporations over individual citizens.\n  - id: 19-67, name: United States v. Sineneng-Smith, description: The case is about whether a federal law that prohibits encouraging or inducing unlawful activity, such as immigration fraud, is too broad and violates the First Amendment right to free speech. The government argues that the law has been interpreted narrowly for decades and is constitutional, while the respondent claims it could be applied to protected speech.\n  - id: 18-1432, name: Nasrallah v. Barr, description: The Supreme Court is deciding whether a specific type of immigration relief, known as CAT relief, can be reviewed by the Court or if Congress has stripped that power away. The issue revolves around a law that limits judicial review of certain immigration orders, and whether this relief fits within the definition of those orders.\n  - id: 19-1414, name: United States v. Cooley, description: The case United States v. Cooley, docket number 19-1414, concerns the inherent authority of Indian tribes to investigate and detain suspects within their borders for violating another sovereign\'s law, with the Ninth Circuit\'s decision potentially limiting tribal sovereignty and policing rights.\n  - id: 22-666, name: Wilkinson v. Garland, Att\'y Gen., description: The case concerns whether courts have the power to review immigration agency decisions denying relief to non-citizens who meet certain criteria, specifically whether the agency\'s application of a legal standard is reviewable under federal law.\n  - id: 18-776, name: Guerrero-Lasprilla v. Barr, description: The case concerns the scope of judicial review over questions of law decided by the Board of Immigration Appeals, specifically whether courts can review whether the government used the proper legal standard in making decisions.\n  - id: 23-1039, name: Ames v. OH Dept. of Youth Services, description: The case, Ames v. OH Dept. of Youth Services, concerns whether a judge-made rule known as the \'background circumstances rule\' is consistent with Title VII of the Civil Rights Act, which aims to eradicate workplace discrimination. The rule in question requires courts to apply a categorical evidentiary presumption against majority-group plaintiffs, potentially allowing discrimination based on protected characteristics. The Court is asked to reverse a lower court\'s judgment that upheld this rule.\n  - id: 23-477, name: United States v. Skrmetti, description: This case is about a Tennessee law that bans certain medications for minors to treat gender dysphoria, with the state arguing it\'s necessary to protect adolescent health.\n\nFollow these rules:\n- Extract parameters strictly according to the schema.\n- Apply the extraction instructions above.\n- Use context only when the user refers indirectly (e.g., "it", "that one").\n- If a parameter cannot be extracted, set it to null.\n- Return ONLY valid JSON. No commentary.\n\nReturn JSON in this format:\n{\n    "parameters": {\n        <param_name>: <value or null>\n    },\n    "reasoning": "<brief explanation>"\n}\n',)
